    logP = -0.2);
  annotation (Documentation(info = "<html><body><p>Enoxacin is a fluoroquinolone antibiotic used to treat bacterial infections, including urinary tract infections, gonorrhea, and infections caused by gram-negative bacteria such as E. coli and Pseudomonas. It has been approved as a systemic antibacterial medicine, though it is not authorised in the European Union and is now used only in some countries.</p><p><small>Summary written by glm-5.3-flash from <a href=&quot;https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1639616&quot;>Wikidata Q1639616</a> and the WHO ATC classification; not checked by a person.</small></p><table><tr><td>name:</td><td>enoxacin</td></tr><tr><td>ATC codes:</td><td>J01MA04</td></tr><tr><td>DrugBank:</td><td><a href=&quot;https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00467&quot;>DB00467</a></td></tr><tr><td>PubChem CID:</td><td><a href=&quot;https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/3229&quot;>3229</a></td></tr><tr><td>ChEBI:</td><td><a href=&quot;https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:157175&quot;>157175</a></td></tr><tr><td>Wikidata:</td><td><a href=&quot;https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1639616&quot;>Q1639616</a></td></tr><tr><td>formula:</td><td>C15H17FN4O3</td></tr><tr><td>molar mass:</td><td>320.324 g/mol (PubChem)</td></tr></table></body></html>"));
